```{r, include = FALSE}
# This vignette is precomputed: it needs a model, so it is knitted locally by
# data-raw/knit_vignettes.R and the output is what you are reading.
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>")
options(width = 90)
```

layaR runs [Laya](https://huggingface.co/convaiinnovations/laya) decision
models on your own machine. You ask typed questions about a piece of text
(a support ticket, an email, a chat message, a prompt) and get calibrated
answers back:

* **choice**: pick one label from a fixed set of options;
* **score**: place the text on an ordinal scale;
* **noul** (yes/no): the probability that a statement holds.

All the questions about one text are answered in a single forward pass, so
asking five questions costs about as much as asking one. Prediction runs on
the CPU through ONNX Runtime; no Python, server or API key is involved.

## Getting a model

layaR needs an ONNX export of a Laya checkpoint. Download one once (1.3 to
1.6 GB):

```{r, eval = FALSE}
library(layaR)
laya_download_model("multilingual")
```

There are three checkpoints:

| Checkpoint | Base encoder | Use it for |
|---|---|---|
| `multilingual` | mmBERT-base | text in any of 100+ languages (the default) |
| `english` | ModernBERT-large | English text; slightly more accurate there |
| `typed-decisions` | ModernBERT-large | four specific workflows it was fine-tuned on |

`laya_models()` shows what is installed:

```{r}
library(layaR)
m <- laya_models()
m$path <- sub(path.expand("~"), "~", m$path, fixed = TRUE)
m
```

`vignette("models-and-performance", package = "layaR")` covers where
models live, importing your own export, memory and threads.

## Asking questions

Build a question set with `laya_questions()`. Each named argument is one
question, and the name becomes the answer's id:

```{r}
questions <- laya_questions(
  department = laya_choice(
    "Which department should handle this request?",
    billing   = "invoices, payments, refunds",
    technical = "bugs, outages, system errors",
    sales     = "pricing, new contracts",
    other     = "everything else"
  ),
  urgency = laya_score(
    "How urgent is this request?",
    c("not urgent", "soon", "critical deadline or blocking issue")
  ),
  churn_risk = laya_noul("Does the user threaten to cancel or leave?")
)
questions
```

A state is the text the model reads. It can be a single string, or a named
list whose field names give the model context:

```{r}
state <- list(
  subject = "Duplicate charge on invoice #4411",
  body = paste("Hi, we were billed twice for March.",
               "Please refund the duplicate today or we will cancel our plan.")
)
```

`laya_predict()` answers every question at once. The first call loads the
model, which takes a few seconds; the engine is then kept for the session.

```{r}
res <- laya_predict(state, questions)
res
```

Two of the answers are right: the ticket is for billing, and it is fairly
urgent. The third is wrong. The message ends with "or we will cancel our
plan", yet the model puts the probability of a cancellation threat at
`r sprintf("%.2f", res$answers$churn_risk$noul)`, with confidence
`r sprintf("%.2f", res$answers$churn_risk$confidence)`. A conditional threat
phrased as a request is the kind of wording yes/no questions can miss.

It is a useful reminder: confidence is calibrated on the data the
checkpoints were trained on, not on yours, and a confident answer can still
be wrong. Before acting on answers automatically, check a few dozen labelled
examples from your own workflow.

When a question misses, reword it. Here the word "threaten" is the problem;
asking whether the customer *says* they will cancel gets it right:

```{r}
reworded <- laya_questions(churn_risk = laya_noul("Does the customer say they will cancel or leave?"))
laya_predict(state, reworded)
```

`vignette("writing-questions", package = "layaR")` has more on wording.

## Reading the answers

`as.data.frame()` gives one row per question:

```{r}
as.data.frame(res)
```

* `answer` is the label for a choice question, the expected level for a
  score question (here between 0, "not urgent", and 2, "critical"), and
  `TRUE`/`FALSE` for a yes/no question.
* `value` is the chosen label's probability, the score, or P(true).
* `confidence` is how sure the model is of its answer, from 0 to 1. The
  checkpoints are calibrated: across many answers given with confidence
  0.8, about 80% are right.

`laya_probabilities()` shows the whole distribution, one row per option:

```{r}
laya_probabilities(res)
```

The result is also a plain list in the Python SDK's shape, so you can reach
into it directly:

```{r}
res$answers$department$choice
res$answers$churn_risk$noul
```

## When the model isn't sure

Set `min_confidence` and every answer reports whether it passed the
threshold. An abstained answer is still there; it is flagged so you can
send it to a person instead:

```{r}
vague <- "Hi, quick question about my account."
laya_predict(vague, questions, min_confidence = 0.8) |>
  as.data.frame() |>
  subset(select = c(id, answer, confidence, abstention))
```

## Presets

Five ready-made question sets come from the Python SDK. Each reads one field
of the state:

```{r}
laya_preset_fields()
laya_predict(list(message = "My payment failed twice and nobody answers my emails!"),
             laya_preset("triage"))
```

